{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 897587e628c56b4d94d4b034614a22677263053e on request/public-post-snapshot-repair, artifact 8ee6083509ae6913ffd4e3351db8f92a3719b22c. I pinned every gate and probe to `git rev-parse HEAD` in the same command; the head read 897587e628c56b4d94d4b034614a22677263053e before and after each one, and the implementer's worktree stayed clean throughout.\n\nSTRUCTURE, COMPUTED RATHER THAN ACCEPTED. Merge commit 9f45a81f27c1a4ed5d08b44c6f00eda749c9bd24 has exactly the two named parents, 41c9fc062cd2cb785d372c9cec4ef694da1dba52 and 4b53dc8d940d775a1605f79adbfd5e7aad49ec06. I computed the automatic merge myself: `git merge-tree --write-tree 41c9fc06 4b53dc8d` succeeds without conflict and yields tree 3f112c63ff82c508fef03d6bcaae8bc9bd3e4ec1. Diffing that tree against the merge commit gives exactly two files and two lines: SECURITY.md changes \"Recheck them before the first public push\" to \"Recheck them before every public update\", and public_surface_test.go changes the required fragment to match. Nothing else in the integration departs from what git would have produced on its own. The child commit adds exactly the three disclosed paths: spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main.go, spike/cmd/gitseq-report/main_test.go, spike/SPIKE-RESULTS.md. Both commits are authored Hugh Pyle \u003chughpyle@gmail.com\u003e; the merge carries Rests-On 37deb6d5b1f70aaf03c020ac73bbafbaeaac1c3e and the child carries Rests-On fb3d243f17291cbe0a35fda55709a2fd7c12ec6d, both canonical full IDs naming live events. LICENSE is byte-identical across both parents and the head. `git diff --check` is clean.\n\nEVERY CLAIM IS NOW LOAD-BEARING. I applied 26 mutations, one at a time in a disposable worktree at the exact head, reverting each before the next. All 26 turn the suite red. The one that matters most: replacing \"Do not open a public issue for a security report.\" with \"Do not worry about it.\" — the precise edit that left the suite green in round two — now fails TestPublicRepositorySurface. The post-snapshot claim itself is pinned in both directions: restoring \"Recheck them before the first public push.\" fails. So do the two structural admissions (\"GitHub settings outside this source tree\", \"Source CI cannot guarantee those settings\"), \"GitHub Issues are public\", \"suitable for ordinary use and support questions\", \"technical preview\", \"There are no supported release branches\", the /issues and /security/advisories/new URLs, the README clone URL, the MIT License link, the security policy link, the getting-started clone command, ci.yml's `contents: read`, `persist-credentials: false`, `git diff --exit-code` and its call to the clone gate, the clone gate's own `$head:refs/heads/main` refspec, its sole-ref assertion and its `make -C \"$checkout\" test vet build`, and both root-local ignore patterns. On the spike side, removing either split guard from the case-2 inventory fails TestRebindingEvidencePinsBothSplitVerifierGuards, and so does putting the deleted combined name TestVerifierRejectsRebindingAndTrailerMutation back in their place. That name appears nowhere at this head.\n\nTHE TWO SPLIT GUARDS ARE REAL, NOT DECORATIVE. Replacing the verifier's target binding at internal/kernel/kernel.go:1001 with `if false` turns TestVerifierRejectsIntentReboundToAnotherLog red. Removing the causal-trailer comparison at both of its sites turns TestVerifierRejectsAlteredCausalTrailersWithFreshIdentity red. The evidence table names guards that carry weight.\n\nEXTERNAL STATE, READ DIRECTLY. `gh api repos/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq` returns private false, visibility public, has_issues true, default_branch main, archived false, fork false. `gh api repos/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/private-vulnerability-reporting` returns enabled true. The whole tree names exactly four external URLs — the repository, its .git clone, /issues, and /security/advisories/new — and configuration now backs all four. Nothing on the public surface asserts a bug bounty, a response-time commitment, a support channel that is closed, or a reporting channel that is off. Round one's defect is gone and the admission that replaced it is accurate.\n\nGATES AT THIS HEAD. make vet, test, race, build all pass. npm ci and npm test pass 49/49. make ui and make spike regenerate, and `git diff --exit-code` is clean, so the committed UI embed and the stable spike projection are byte-identical to what the tools produce. .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh passes: the bare fixture carried exactly refs/heads/main at this head, and the fresh clone built, tested and vetted and stayed clean. actionlint v1.7.7 is clean. gitleaks v8.30.1 finds nothing in the working tree and nothing in the exact head's history: scoped to this commit it scanned 129 patch-bearing commits and 8,521,640 bytes and reported no leaks, matching the ready-for-review numbers to the byte. My independent recount of the exact history gives 187 commits, 868 blobs and 21,554,486 blob bytes, with only two reachable identities, Hugh Pyle \u003chughpyle@gmail.com\u003e and claude \u003cclaude@gitseq.invalid\u003e — again matching exactly.\n\nOne note on how I ran that scan, so the number is not misread later. Left unscoped, gitleaks walks every ref in the local repository, and that run reports 1,620 findings over 1,791 commits. I checked where they are rather than assuming. All 1,620 come from the generic-api-key rule, all sit in three commits on refs/seq/5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8 — the workroom event log — and all are in blobs named checkpoint, event, and attachments/discussion.json, which carry the signed envelopes and their Actor-Key and Actor-Signature fields. That is public key material and signatures by construction, and exactly the high-entropy shape a scanner flags. None of those three commits is in this head's history, and the clone gate shows none of it travels on a public push: the bare fixture advertised refs/heads/main and nothing else. The load-bearing scan is the scoped one, and it is clean.\n\nThe strongest unpinned sentence in SECURITY.md is the loopback claim, so I checked it against the code rather than the prose. validateLoopbackListen and validateLoopbackServer in cmd/gs/main.go reject wildcard, non-loopback, non-http and path-bearing addresses, and both are covered by table-driven tests. The claim is true.\n\nTHREE THINGS TO KNOW. None blocks this head.\n\nFirst, main moved during the review. It was 41c9fc062cd2cb785d372c9cec4ef694da1dba52, the first parent, when I started; it is now dfa51178104ea5c2a5c91e81ac72e3f8697b1759, having gained 1d3081c4b7c885e7a13911e5cee3239d1722627a and its merge. The reviewed head is no longer a descendant of main. The paths main gained (cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/app, internal/service, internal/statusview, docs/reference.md) do not intersect the ten this head touches, so re-integration is textually clean and none of the pins I mutated are affected. But request 37deb6d5 plans a force-with-lease convergence of refs/heads/main. Run against this head as it stands, that would drop those two commits from main. The verdict names an immutable commit and stands; the convergence step needs re-planning against the current main before anyone executes it.\n\nSecond, an inherited kernel gap that the spike evidence slightly overstates. Case 2 names TestVerifierRejectsIntentReboundToAnotherLog as evidence that actor intent binds its target. That guard covers the full-scan verifier at internal/kernel/kernel.go:1001. The same binding is enforced a second time on the checkpoint fast path at internal/kernel/checkpoint.go:197, and nothing pins it: I replaced that condition with `if false` and the entire internal/kernel package still passed. TestCheckpointMetadataBindsEnvelopeTrailersAndTreeIndependently covers that path's envelope, causal trailers, commit tree, timestamp, envelope parse and payload tree identity, but not its target/genesis binding. This is inherited from main — this head does not touch internal/kernel — so it is not a reason to withhold approval, but it deserves a follow-up request.\n\nThird, a limit of the method worth stating plainly. The public surface is pinned by substring containment over whitespace-collapsed text. It caught deletion, rewording and reversal in every case I tried, which is what it is for. It cannot catch a sentence that keeps the pinned fragment and appends a clause reversing it. No such prose exists at this head; this is a property of the approach, not a defect of the work.\n\nFor context: the currently published origin/main, 936818f674e2ad5123d3cfa63d56a2ac238a6aa7, carries no SECURITY.md, so nothing false is live on the public repository today — this head is what puts a truthful policy there.\n\nI did not edit, merge, ratify, push, fetch, force, change any repository setting, or mutate any remote ref. The GitHub reads were read-only. Both scratch worktrees have been removed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8ee6083509ae6913ffd4e3351db8f92a3719b22c","head":"897587e628c56b4d94d4b034614a22677263053e","verdict":"approved"}}